%[text] ## 1    Introduction
%[text] In this project, we use a Sugeno fuzzy logic controller to control the same problem, but with slightly different masses and pendulum length as compared to lab 2.
%[text]{"align":"center"} %[text:anchor:M_AF2A433C] ![](text:image:5520)
%[text] The worksheet is organized as follows:
%[text] - Section 2 requires you to define the random seed and tune parameters.
%[text] - Section 3 requires you to build the Mamdani fuzzy inference system.
%[text] - Section 4 requires you to define the controller to balance the pendulum.
%[text] - Section 5 simulates the system with the controller, and outputs the responses.
%[text] - Section 6 allows you to export and figures that are generated by the worksheet.
%[text] - Section 7 includes deadlines and submission requirements.
%[text] - Section 8 shows how analysis can be done. \
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_8D0CC03F] ## 2    Definitions
%[text] In this section, we define parameters and construct the system.
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_054C62D4] ### 2.1    Initializations of Constants
%[text] 1. Replace the numbers with the numeric digits of your team's matric numbers. If your team's matric numbers are A0123456A and A0987654B, then the line should be `rng(sum([123456, 987654]))`. \
clear all; close all; clc; format compact
rng(sum([286716, 284748])); % !TODO (1)
 
constants = struct();
constants.G = 9.8;
constants.M_CART = rand() * 1 + 0.5; % cart mass, kg
constants.M_PEND = rand() * 0.2 + 1.4; % pend mass, kg
constants.L_PEND = rand() * 0.2 + 0.9; % pend length, m
constants.PHI = 30; % slope angle, deg
constants.GAMMA_SET = 1; % settling angle, deg
constants.START_GAMMAS = [-10, -5, 5, 10]; % deg
constants.F_UNI = [-inf, inf];
constants.D_UNI = [-inf, inf];
constants.P_UNI = [-inf, inf];

fprintf("Slope=%.3f deg; Cart mass=%.3f kg; Pend mass=%.3f kg; Pend length=%.3f m\n", constants.PHI, constants.M_CART, constants.M_PEND, constants.L_PEND); %[output:1f5b7d24]
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_624C4995] ### 2.2    Find Weight Along Slope
%[text] Find the magnitude of the force pointing down the slope that is contributed by the weight of the system. This force is parallel to and pointing down the slope, and may be useful for tuning. Let this be stored in the `constants.WEIGHT`. To calculate the force, the following constants may be used:
%[text] - `constants.G`: Acceleration due to gravity, in m/s/s.
%[text] - `constants.PHI`: the slope angle, in ***degrees***.
%[text] - `constants.M_CART`: the mass of the cart, in kg
%[text] - `constants.M_PEND`: the mass of the pendulum, in kg. \
constants.WEIGHT = constants.G * sind(constants.PHI) * (constants.M_CART + constants.M_PEND); % force down the slope, N
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_2915BF02] ### 2.3    Universes of Discourse
%[text] Define the universe of discourse for...
%[text] 1. the proportional error, which is between -20 and 20 (deg) inclusive, to be stored in `constants.P_UNI`.
%[text] 2. the derivative error, which is between -80 and 80 (deg/s) inclusive, to be stored in `constants.D_UNI`.
%[text] 3. the output force, which is between **-50 and 50** (N) inclusive, to be stored in `constants.F_UNI`. This is different from lab 2 as crisp values are now used. \
%[text] Ignore the units when assigning the arrays.
constants.P_UNI = [-20, 20]; % proportional error universe, deg
constants.D_UNI = [-80, 80]; % derivative error universe, deg/s
constants.F_UNI = [-50, 50]; % output force universe, N
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_8D15CFB5] ### 2.4    Membership Functions
%[text] Like in lab 2, the membership functions (MFs) are defined to be trapezoids, and are controlled by the parameters $a$, $b$, $c$, and $d$. 
%[text] Unlike lab 2 where the Mamdani fuzzy inference system is used, in project 1 we use the Sugeno fuzzy inference system. We need to define 5 membership functions for the input variables *proportional error* and *derivative error*, and define 5 crisp values for the output variable *output force*.
%[text] 1. For the input variable *proportional error*, there are five terms negative-big (NB), negative-small (NS), near-zero (ZO), positive-small (PS), and positive-big (PB). Each term is described by a trapezoidal MF, and defined in `params.P_NB`, `params.P_NS`, `params.P_ZO`, `params.P_PS`, and `params.P_PB`, respectively. The proportional error is in (deg).
%[text] 2. For the input variable *derivative error*, do the same. The parameters are defined in `params.D_NB`, `params.D_NS`, `params.D_ZO`, `params.D_PS`, and `params.D_PB`. The derivative error is in (deg/s).
%[text] 3. For the output variable *output force*, assign a ***crisp*** value to each term. The parameters are defined in `params.F_NB`, `params.F_NS`, `params.F_ZO`, `params.F_PS`, and `params.F_PB`. The output force is in (N). \
% parameters for all trapmf 
params = struct();
% Base-line model (Section 8.1): proportional and derivative error parameters
% from lab 2, with the two additional terms distributed evenly around them.
% proportional error MFs (deg), inner node at +/-2.6 from lab 2
params.P_NB = [-20, -20, -10, -2.6];
params.P_NS = [-10, -2.6, -2.6, 0];
params.P_ZO = [-2.6, 0, 0, 2.6];
params.P_PS = [0, 2.6, 2.6, 10];
params.P_PB = [2.6, 10, 20, 20];
% derivative error MFs (deg/s), inner node at +/-35 from lab 2
params.D_NB = [-80, -80, -60, -35];
params.D_NS = [-60, -35, -35, 0];
params.D_ZO = [-35, 0, 0, 35];
params.D_PS = [0, 35, 35, 60];
params.D_PB = [35, 60, 80, 80];
% output force crisp values (N), evenly distributed over the universe
params.F_NB = -50;
params.F_NS = -25;
params.F_ZO = 0;
params.F_PS = 25;
params.F_PB = 50;
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_CE38DB1F] ## 3    The Fuzzy Inference System
%[text] Initialize the system, but this time using `sugfis()`. For this section, adapt the code from lab 2.
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_31283119] ### 3.1    Input and Output Variables
%[text] 1. Add input and output variables to the fuzzy system `fis` using `addInput()` and `addOutput()`. \
function fis = initFis(constants, params)
fis = sugfis('Name', 'p1');

fis = addInput(fis, constants.P_UNI, 'Name', 'p');
fis = addInput(fis, constants.D_UNI, 'Name', 'd');
fis = addOutput(fis, constants.F_UNI, 'Name', 'f');
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_AC7420A5] ### 3.2    Attach Membership Functions to Variables
%[text] 1. Register the membership functions to each variable. You may need to look up `addMf()` to figure out how to add the crisp force values. \
fis = addMF(fis, 'p', 'trapmf', params.P_NB, 'Name', 'NB');
fis = addMF(fis, 'p', 'trapmf', params.P_NS, 'Name', 'NS');
fis = addMF(fis, 'p', 'trapmf', params.P_ZO, 'Name', 'ZO');
fis = addMF(fis, 'p', 'trapmf', params.P_PS, 'Name', 'PS');
fis = addMF(fis, 'p', 'trapmf', params.P_PB, 'Name', 'PB');
fis = addMF(fis, 'd', 'trapmf', params.D_NB, 'Name', 'NB');
fis = addMF(fis, 'd', 'trapmf', params.D_NS, 'Name', 'NS');
fis = addMF(fis, 'd', 'trapmf', params.D_ZO, 'Name', 'ZO');
fis = addMF(fis, 'd', 'trapmf', params.D_PS, 'Name', 'PS');
fis = addMF(fis, 'd', 'trapmf', params.D_PB, 'Name', 'PB');
fis = addMF(fis, 'f', 'constant', params.F_NB, 'Name', 'NB');
fis = addMF(fis, 'f', 'constant', params.F_NS, 'Name', 'NS');
fis = addMF(fis, 'f', 'constant', params.F_ZO, 'Name', 'ZO');
fis = addMF(fis, 'f', 'constant', params.F_PS, 'Name', 'PS');
fis = addMF(fis, 'f', 'constant', params.F_PB, 'Name', 'PB');
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_865FFB12] ### 3.3    Apply Fuzzy Rules to Variables
%[text] 1. Program the $5\\times5$ rules using `addRule()`. To minimize semantic errors, make use of the comments to tabulate the rules. \
% You may use the following comment block to tabulate the rules,
% so that it is easier to code the rules.
%       d=NB    d=NS    d=ZO    d=PS    d=PB
% p=NB   NB      NB      NB      NS      ZO
% p=NS   NB      NB      NS      ZO      PS
% p=ZO   NB      NS      ZO      PS      PB
% p=PS   NS      ZO      PS      PB      PB
% p=PB   ZO      PS      PB      PB      PB

fis = addRule(fis, 'p==NB & d==NB => f==NB');
fis = addRule(fis, 'p==NB & d==NS => f==NB');
fis = addRule(fis, 'p==NB & d==ZO => f==NB');
fis = addRule(fis, 'p==NB & d==PS => f==NS');
fis = addRule(fis, 'p==NB & d==PB => f==ZO');
fis = addRule(fis, 'p==NS & d==NB => f==NB');
fis = addRule(fis, 'p==NS & d==NS => f==NB');
fis = addRule(fis, 'p==NS & d==ZO => f==NS');
fis = addRule(fis, 'p==NS & d==PS => f==ZO');
fis = addRule(fis, 'p==NS & d==PB => f==PS');
fis = addRule(fis, 'p==ZO & d==NB => f==NB');
fis = addRule(fis, 'p==ZO & d==NS => f==NS');
fis = addRule(fis, 'p==ZO & d==ZO => f==ZO');
fis = addRule(fis, 'p==ZO & d==PS => f==PS');
fis = addRule(fis, 'p==ZO & d==PB => f==PB');
fis = addRule(fis, 'p==PS & d==NB => f==NS');
fis = addRule(fis, 'p==PS & d==NS => f==ZO');
fis = addRule(fis, 'p==PS & d==ZO => f==PS');
fis = addRule(fis, 'p==PS & d==PS => f==PB');
fis = addRule(fis, 'p==PS & d==PB => f==PB');
fis = addRule(fis, 'p==PB & d==NB => f==ZO');
fis = addRule(fis, 'p==PB & d==NS => f==PS');
fis = addRule(fis, 'p==PB & d==ZO => f==PB');
fis = addRule(fis, 'p==PB & d==PS => f==PB');
fis = addRule(fis, 'p==PB & d==PB => f==PB');
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_77FB4D5C] ### 3.4    Fuzzy Methods
%[text] Ensure that the methods used by the Sugeno fuzzy inference system is the same as the lecture:
%[text] 1. `AndMethod`, for implementing the *and* condition in the antecedent.
%[text] 2. `OrMethod`, for implementing the *or* condition in the antecedent.
%[text] 3. `ImplicationMethod`, for the implication step, or the shape of the consequent MF from the firing strength for each rule.
%[text] 4. `AggregationMethod`, for the aggregation step, which is to combine all of the consequent MFs from all rules.
%[text] 5. `DefuzzificationMethod`, which is to find the defuzzified crisp value from the aggregated MF. \
fis.AndMethod = 'min';
fis.OrMethod = 'max';
fis.ImplicationMethod = 'prod'; % Sugeno systems only support 'prod' implication
fis.AggregationMethod = 'sum';
fis.DefuzzificationMethod = 'wtaver';

end %function end
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_5FF2C10F] ### 3.5    Plot Membership Functions and Output Surface
%[text] Run the code in section 2 using `Ctrl+Enter`. Fix any errors that show up.
fis = initFis(constants, params); % call the function %[text:anchor:TMP_9fa7]

% plot the MFs
function fig1 = plotFuzzyInferenceSystem(fis)
fig1 = figure(1);
tld = tiledlayout(1, 4, 'TileSpacing', 'Tight');
title(tld, "Proj1 Membership Functions (Sugeno Fuzzy PD Control 5×5)");
fig1.Position = [20, 20, 1600, 360];

nexttile;
plotmf(fis, 'input', 1, 1000);
grid on;

nexttile;
plotmf(fis, 'input', 2, 1000);
grid on;

nexttile;
plot([fis.Outputs(1).MembershipFunctions.Parameters], ...
    zeros(1, numel([fis.Outputs(1).MembershipFunctions.Parameters])), ...
    'or'...
    );
set(gca, 'YColor', 'None')
set(gca, 'XAxisLocation', 'origin')
xlabel("f")
force_params = [fis.Outputs(1).MembershipFunctions.Parameters];
xticks(force_params)
xlim([min(force_params)-10, max(force_params)+10])
grid on;

nexttile;
gensurf(fis);
end

fig1 = plotFuzzyInferenceSystem(fis); %[output:254007f1]
set(fig1, 'Visible', 'on') %[output:254007f1]
%%
%[text] ## 4    Controller
%[text] The angle $\\gamma$ (deg) and its velocity $\\dot{\\gamma}$ (deg/s) are stored in `gamma` and `gamma_vel` respectively. In the function `controller()`, implement the controller using the fuzzy inference system `fis` to output a force `f` to move the cart. 
%[text] 1. Calculate the proportional error `p`. It is the desired angle subtracted by the current angle `gamma`.
%[text] 2. Calculate the derivative error `d`. It is the desired angular velocity subtracted by the current angular velocity `gamma_vel`.
%[text] 3. Using `evalfis()`, calculate the force `f` that is output by the fuzzy inference system `fis` using the inputs `p` and `d`. \
function f = controller(fis, gamma, gamma_vel) %[text:anchor:TMP_5ccb]
p = 0 - gamma; % (1) proportional error, desired angle - current angle
d = 0 - gamma_vel; % (2) derivative error, desired velocity - current velocity
f = evalfis(fis, [p, d]); % (3) crisp force from the Sugeno FIS

end
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_0A9A0668] ## 5    Simulation
%[text] By tuning the membership functions and rules, ensure that the following requirements are met:
%[text] 1. Have a settling time of 1s or less for all starting angles $\\gamma\_0 \\in\\{-10^\\circ, -5^\\circ, 5^\\circ, 10^\\circ\\}$. The settling time can be seen in the figure as $t\_s$.
%[text] 2. Have negligible overshoot in $\\gamma$.
%[text] 3. Steady state $\\gamma$ should be as close to zero as possible. Hint: adjust the force MFs.
%[text] 4. Negligible oscillations in all responses.
%[text] 5. Ignore the cart's displacement and velocity. \
%[text] We will leave it to you to figure out the tuning process.
%[text] <u>**Just run. Ignore the code in this section.**</u> The code uses Euler Lagrange formulation to simulate the system.
function response = simulate(fis, constants, start_gamma)

% start_gamma is in degrees.
DT = 0.01; % simulate frequency in seconds.
DT2 = DT * DT;
T_TOTAL = 5; % in seconds
CTRL_STEP = 5; % 5*DT is controller frequency.
KF_CART = 3; % cart friction coeff
KF_PEND = 1e-4; % pend friction coeff
S_PHI = sind(constants.PHI);
C_PHI = cosd(constants.PHI);
M_TOTAL = constants.M_CART + constants.M_PEND;
L76 = -constants.L_PEND * 7.0 / 6.0;
MML76 = M_TOTAL * L76;
WEIGHT = constants.G * S_PHI * M_TOTAL;

T = 0:DT:T_TOTAL; %sim for 0.01s for 10s
PS = nan(size(T)); % gamma
PV = nan(size(T));
F = nan(size(T));
f = 0;
theta = deg2rad(start_gamma - constants.PHI);
pv = 0;
cs = 0;
cv = 0;
for i = 1:numel(T)
    C_THETA = cos(theta);
    S_THETA = sin(theta);
    % From euler lagrange formulation
    tmp_a = f - 0.5*pv*pv*S_THETA - WEIGHT - cv*KF_CART;
    tmp_b = -constants.G * (S_THETA*C_PHI + C_THETA*S_PHI) - pv*KF_PEND;
    tmp_denom = MML76 - 0.5*C_THETA*C_THETA;
    ca = (L76*tmp_a + 0.5*C_THETA*tmp_b) / tmp_denom;
    pa = (-C_THETA*tmp_a + M_TOTAL*tmp_b) / tmp_denom;
    % linear approximation
    theta = theta + pv*DT + 0.5*pa*DT2;
    ps = theta + deg2rad(constants.PHI);
    pv = pv + pa*DT;
    cs = cs + cv*DT + 0.5*ca*DT2;
    cv = cv + ca*DT;
    % controller
    if mod(i-1, CTRL_STEP) == 0
        % ps = ps + randn()*1e-4; % corrupt with noise
        % pv = pv + randn()*1e-4; % corrupt with noise
        % controller
        f = controller(fis, rad2deg(wrapToPi(ps)), rad2deg(pv));
        % saturate force like real world system
        f = clip(f, -50, 50);
    end
    PS(i) = ps;
    PV(i) = pv;
    F(i) = f;
end

response.gamma = rad2deg(wrapToPi(PS));
response.gamma_vel = rad2deg(PV);
response.force = F;
response.time = T;
end

% ================= function to analyze / evaluate response
function  response = evalResponse(response, constants)
    idx = find(flip(response.gamma < -constants.GAMMA_SET | response.gamma > constants.GAMMA_SET), 1, "first"); % flip the ps, find the first index when the response is outside of settling gamma. 
    if ~isempty(idx) && idx > 1 % has settling time. 
        response.settling_time = response.time(numel(response.gamma) - idx + 2);
    else
        response.settling_time = inf;
    end
end

% ================= run the simulation for all START_GAMMAS
responses = [];
for i = 1:numel(constants.START_GAMMAS)
    r = simulate(fis, constants, constants.START_GAMMAS(i));
    r = evalResponse(r, constants);
    responses = [responses, r];
end

% ===================== plot
function fig2 = plotResponses(responses, constants)
fig2 = figure(2);
fig2.Position = [0, 0, 1200, 720];
tld = tiledlayout(numel(constants.START_GAMMAS), 3, TileSpacing="tight");
title(tld, "Proj1 Responses (Sugeno Fuzzy PD Control 5×5)");
xlabel(tld, "Time (s)");
for i = 1:numel(constants.START_GAMMAS)
    r = responses(i);

    nexttile
    plot(r.time, r.gamma)
    start_gamma = constants.START_GAMMAS(i);
    title(sprintf('Gamma (Initial=%.2f)', start_gamma));
    if (~isinf(r.settling_time))
        xline(r.settling_time, '--k');
        text(r.settling_time, sign(start_gamma) * constants.GAMMA_SET, sprintf('t_s = %.3fs', r.settling_time))
    end
    grid on
    xlim([-0.1, r.time(end)])
    ylabel("$\gamma$ (deg)", Interpreter="latex");

    nexttile
    plot(r.time, r.gamma_vel)
    title('Gamma Velocity');
    grid on
    xlim([-0.1, r.time(end)])
    ylabel("$\dot{\gamma}$ (deg/s)", Interpreter="latex");

    nexttile
    plot(r.time, r.force)
    title('Cart Force');
    grid on
    xlim([-0.1, r.time(end)])
    ylabel("$F$ (N)", Interpreter="latex");
end
end

fig2 = plotResponses(responses, constants); %[output:81aaef3f]
set(fig2, 'Visible', 'on') %[output:81aaef3f]

